A||||4||merlin||Reconditioning V6 Cylinder Heads.  What needs to be done?…||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||10-23-2001||08:36 PM||||I’ve recently removed the cylinder heads from my 1987 V6 GT due to a blown head gasket and I was hoping to get a little advice on what reconditioning is a ‘do-it-yourself’ job and what is best left to the machine shop.<P>Mill the heads? – I used a feeler gauge and a straight edge on both cylinders and there doesn’t appear to be more than a .003 deviation when I measured at various points.  However, I had one mechanic tell me that it has to be done in all cases.  Is this true?…  <P>Valves? – Is this a difficult do-it-yourself job?  How high is the ‘screw up’ factor?<P>Crack test? – Isn’t there a spray on chemical that can be used to find cracks in the cylinder head?<P>Thanks for any help.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fiero_24||10-23-2001||08:50 PM||||I had my heads done at a shop.  I had a 3 way valve job done, and bowl porting.  I did have new seals, and crane springs put on also.  I am not sure what you can do on you own.  I am not very handy so I left it all to the pro's.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||10
Z||000002||DreXteR||10-23-2001||10:26 PM||||I really sugest take it to the shop. If you do something wrong, you will need to buy a new head and take it to the shop anyway. The only thing you can do is the porting, look for some manuals and get a dremel. Valves, seals and so leave it to the shop.<P>DreX<P>------------------<BR>          <IMG SRC="http://users.centennialpr.net/drexter/fierofire1.jpg">         <BR>  <BR>Just another Red Fiero - Back 2 the Future Prototype.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by DreXteR (edited 10-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||10-24-2001||10:33 AM||||Merlin,  You should have a shop do them as mentioned above.  They have the proper tools for crack detection and machining.  Just shop around for reputation and pricing.  You don't want to attempt it yourself, put them back on and then take them off and have a shop do them again.  Have the springs checked & replaced if necessary.  Have the valve guides checked and replaced if necessary.  Of course a quality valve job and seal replacement.  I would suggest having the heads touched, but as minimal as possible.  I had mine done for $235.  That was 4 valves replaced, new guides, valve job, checked for cracks, touched the mating surface and valve job cleaning etc.  End result is improved performance and no leaks or burning oil.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000004||samoht||10-25-2001||09:47 PM||||i usta work at a rebuild shop and I did my own heads.. if you want to port your heads yourself go for it.. DONT TOUCH THE VEIN IN THE MIDDLE OF THE PORT.. DO NOT GRIND TO CLOSE TO THE VAVLE SEATS !!!  Although I think that the fiero exhaust sucks so much that porting isnt really going to help.. look inside the exhaust manifolds and you will see what I mean..  Mill the head .015in. and have a 5 angle vavle job done.. but a 3 in ok..   $10 to check for cracks.. if you need no new parts and without the porting you are looking a about $150...if you dont plan to race it or run at high RPM.. just shim the vavle springs and new positive seals will come with the head kit....  They made a large and small valve 2.8 steal heads.. you might want to make sure you have the large valve first or I would just get a standard vavle job<P>85gt3.2||||reg||1
